      <h1>How to Handle an Inherited House With a Mortgage in Texas</h1>
      <p class="hero-sub">A mortgage doesn't disappear when someone passes away. Here's what happens to it, who's responsible, and what your options are as an heir in Texas.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="container article-layout">
      <article class="article-body">

        <div class="article-intro">
          <p>One of the first questions families ask when they inherit a property is what happens to the mortgage. The short answer is that the loan doesn't go away. But the longer answer is more nuanced -- and your options as an heir are broader than most people realize. This guide walks through how mortgage debt is handled when a property is inherited in Texas, and what paths are available to you.</p>
        </div>

        <h2>What Happens to the Mortgage When Someone Dies?</h2>
        <p>When a homeowner passes away, their mortgage doesn't disappear with them. The loan remains attached to the property and becomes a liability of the estate. Someone has to keep up with payments, or the lender will eventually begin the foreclosure process.</p>
        <p>That said, federal law offers some protection to heirs. The Garn-St. Germain Depository Institutions Act prevents lenders from triggering a due-on-sale clause -- the provision that would normally require the loan to be paid in full when ownership transfers -- when a property is inherited by a family member. In plain terms, this means that as an heir, you can take over the property and continue making payments on the existing mortgage without the lender being able to demand the full balance immediately.</p>
        <p>This is an important protection that many families don't know about. You have more time and more options than it might feel like in the immediate aftermath of a loss.</p>

        <h2>Who Is Responsible for the Payments?</h2>
        <p>During probate, the estate is responsible for keeping up with mortgage payments. The executor manages the estate's finances, which includes making sure the mortgage doesn't fall behind while the legal process plays out.</p>
        <p>If the estate doesn't have liquid assets to cover the payments, this can create pressure to resolve the property quickly. That's one of the most common reasons families in probate situations choose to sell sooner rather than later -- carrying costs like a mortgage, property taxes, and insurance on a vacant property add up fast.</p>

        <div class="callout-box">
          <h4>What about mortgage insurance?</h4>
          <p>Some mortgages carry mortgage life insurance, a separate policy that pays off the loan balance if the borrower dies. If you're not sure whether this was in place, check with the lender or look through the deceased's financial documents. If a policy exists, it could eliminate the mortgage balance entirely and significantly simplify your options.</p>
        </div>

        <h2>Your Options as an Heir</h2>
        <p>Once you understand the mortgage situation, there are several paths forward depending on what makes sense for your family.</p>

        <div class="process-steps">
          <div class="process-step">
            <div class="step-label">Option 1</div>
            <h4>Continue making payments and keep the property</h4>
            <p>If you want to keep the home -- to live in, rent out, or hold as an investment -- you can continue making payments on the existing loan. Federal law allows heirs to assume the mortgage without triggering the due-on-sale clause. You'll want to contact the lender to notify them of the death, update the account information, and get clear on the terms. Eventually you may want to refinance the loan into your own name, which requires you to qualify based on your own credit and income.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="process-step">
            <div class="step-label">Option 2</div>
            <h4>Sell the property and pay off the mortgage at closing</h4>
            <p>This is the most straightforward path for most families who don't want to keep the home. The property sells, the mortgage balance gets paid off from the proceeds at closing, and the remaining equity is distributed to the heirs. As long as the home is worth more than the mortgage balance, there will be equity to distribute. The mortgage being present doesn't block a sale -- it's just resolved at closing like it would be in any normal transaction.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="process-step">
            <div class="step-label">Option 3</div>
            <h4>Refinance into your own name</h4>
            <p>If you want to keep the property long-term, refinancing into your own name gives you a fresh loan with current rates and clear ownership. This requires you to qualify based on your own financial profile. Depending on current interest rates compared to the existing mortgage rate, this might or might not make financial sense. Worth running the numbers before committing.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="process-step">
            <div class="step-label">Option 4</div>
            <h4>Sell to a cash buyer</h4>
            <p>If the timeline is tight, the property needs work, or you want to avoid the full listing process, a cash sale can resolve everything quickly. The mortgage gets paid at closing just like it would in a traditional sale, and the transaction doesn't depend on a buyer's financing coming through. For families managing probate timelines and carrying costs, this is often the cleanest path.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h2>What If the Mortgage Balance Is More Than the Home Is Worth?</h2>
        <p>This is called being underwater or upside-down on the mortgage, and it does happen with inherited properties -- especially older loans, properties in declining areas, or homes that have suffered deferred maintenance.</p>
        <p>In this situation, a standard sale won't generate enough to pay off the loan, which means the estate can't distribute any equity to heirs. A few paths exist:</p>

        <div class="qa-list">
          <div class="qa-item">
            <h4>Short sale</h4>
            <p>A short sale is when the lender agrees to accept less than the full mortgage balance as payment in full. The property is sold, the lender takes the proceeds, and the remaining deficiency is forgiven. Lenders aren't required to agree to a short sale, and the process requires their cooperation, but it's an option worth exploring if the property is significantly underwater.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="qa-item">
            <h4>Deed in lieu of foreclosure</h4>
            <p>The estate transfers the property title directly to the lender in exchange for releasing the mortgage obligation. This avoids the formal foreclosure process and can be faster and cleaner when the numbers simply don't work for a traditional sale.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="qa-item">
            <h4>Allow foreclosure</h4>
            <p>If no heir wants the property and the estate has no other assets, allowing foreclosure is sometimes the practical outcome. As an heir, you're generally not personally liable for the mortgage -- the debt is attached to the property and the estate, not to you individually, unless you co-signed the original loan.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h2>What About a Reverse Mortgage?</h2>
        <p>Reverse mortgages work differently and come with their own set of rules for heirs. When the borrower passes away, the loan typically becomes due within a set period -- usually around 30 days for notification and up to six months to resolve it. Heirs can sell the property, pay off the reverse mortgage balance and keep the home, or in some cases deed it back to the lender.</p>
        <p>We've covered reverse mortgages in detail in a separate post. If that's your situation, it's worth reading before you take any action.</p>

        <h2>First Steps When You Inherit a Mortgaged Property</h2>

        <div class="process-steps">
          <div class="process-step">
            <div class="step-label">Step 1</div>
            <h4>Notify the lender</h4>
            <p>Contact the mortgage servicer as soon as possible after the death. You'll need a copy of the death certificate. Ask for the current loan balance, the payoff amount, and information about any grace periods or forbearance options. Get everything in writing.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="process-step">
            <div class="step-label">Step 2</div>
            <h4>Keep the payments current if possible</h4>
            <p>A delinquent mortgage creates additional complications and costs. If the estate has funds to cover payments, keep them current while you figure out the longer-term plan.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="process-step">
            <div class="step-label">Step 3</div>
            <h4>Get an independent valuation of the property</h4>
            <p>Before deciding what to do, understand what the home is actually worth in today's market. That number, compared to the mortgage balance, will clarify which options make financial sense.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="process-step">
            <div class="step-label">Step 4</div>
            <h4>Talk to a probate attorney</h4>
            <p>The mortgage situation needs to be handled within the context of the probate process. An attorney can confirm who has authority to make decisions about the property and what the legal obligations are for the estate.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="process-step">
            <div class="step-label">Step 5</div>
            <h4>Understand your real estate options</h4>
            <p>Once you know the loan balance and the property value, the real estate decision becomes clearer. Whether that's a market listing, a cash sale, or keeping the home, we can walk you through what each path looks like in practice.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <p>We've helped families across DFW navigate inherited properties with mortgages at every stage of this process. The mortgage is almost never the dealbreaker people fear it will be -- it just needs to be factored into the plan from the start.</p>

